  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nSunday - Wednesday...\n\nUpper ridging builds from the central CONUS to the eastern CONUS \nfrom late weekend through the middle of next week. This will lead to \na more prolonged period of dry weather compared to what we have seen \nmost of this summer. In addition, temperatures will be on the \nincrease, especially as we get into next week. Forecast soundings \ndon't necessarily show much of a cap, however they do show pretty \ndry low levels with abnormally high LCL/LFC heights, and with the \nlack of any triggers outside of diurnal heating it does look like we \nstay largely dry. Could we pop a stray shower or brief storm one \nafternoon due to the lack of a cap? Sure, but coverage/confidence \nare way too low to carry any mention at this time. \n\nThe bigger issue will be temperatures steadily heating up \nthanks to the building ridge. Highs are expected mostly in the \nlow 90s on Sunday, and then will give way to readings in the low\nand mid 90s at least through Wednesday. Dew points are mostly \nexpected to be in the upper 60s and low 70s through this period,\nand while that will be muggy, it won't be to the extent as some\nof the earlier stretches of heat and humidity we saw this \nsummer. As a result, max heat indices are largely expected to \nstay in the 90s on Sunday and Monday, with values more likely to\nreach into the low 100s by Tuesday and Wednesday. It is \npossible we could flirt with some Heat Advisory headlines by \nthis time, but that would be mainly along or west of I- 65.\n\nThursday - Friday...\n\nThere is lower confidence through the late week time frame whether \nor not the upper ridge will remain in place over our region, or \nwhether it will retrograde back to the west and allow for the \nstronger NW flow aloft to regain dominance over our upper pattern. \nThis will ultimately decide whether we stay in the increasingly hot \nand mostly dry pattern, or whether we get into a more active pattern \nwith convection. The latter scenario would likely result in some \nslightly lower temperatures, but also would come with higher storm \nchances. For now, will continue with a blend of those two scenarios \nwhere temperatures still stay pretty warm/muggy, but also with some \nscattered shower and storm chances each day.",
